{"id":"orcl-earnings-fq1-fy2027","title":"ORCL earnings — Oracle, fiscal Q1 FY2027","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-09-10","html":"<p>Management sees AI demand growing faster than it can build and raised its full-year targets, and the thing that could spoil it is that the build runs on borrowed money, share sales and leases while a few AI customers make up much of the backlog.</p>\n<p><strong>This report is two months old. Oracle reports again around Dec 14, 2026, and it has held an investor day and raised $20B since.</strong></p>\n<p>Reported 2026-09-10. Next report: about 2026-12-14 (Webull's calendar; not confirmed by the company). Updated 2026-11-12. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> stronger. Cloud infrastructure revenue +121%; backlog $664B.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> raised. FY27 revenue &quot;at least $90B&quot; (June said $90B); non-GAAP EPS $8.10 from $8.05. Next quarter: revenue growth 30–34%, non-GAAP EPS $1.85–1.93.</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> funding the build. Free cash flow negative $5.4B, $125.0B of notes and borrowings, $288B of leases not yet on the balance sheet.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> leans down. Price $128.38 is under the 50-day and 200-day; my 38.2% line at $132.46 is the first test and the Nov 6 low at $109.21 is the floor.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Revenue $19.35B (+30%) against a $19.13B estimate.</li><li>Non-GAAP EPS $1.92 against $1.73 expected (Newsquawk); GAAP EPS $1.56 against a $1.30 Webull estimate. Beats on both bases; do not compare one basis with the other.</li><li>Operating cash flow $23.1B, helped by $11.4B of customer prepayments. Capital spending $28.5B against $8.5B a year ago.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Full year:</strong> &quot;we are upgrading our guidance to at least $90 billion in total revenues&quot; (Hilary Maxson, CFO; third-party transcript).</li><li><strong>Next quarter:</strong> cloud revenue growth 65–71%.</li><li><strong>Backlog:</strong> $664B, up $26B in the quarter and $209B in a year, with more than $30B of new AI contracts. About half converts within 36 months.</li><li><strong>Pricing:</strong> GPU utilisation near 98%, and renewed GPU contracts priced about 20% above the originals (per recaps).</li><li><strong>Supply:</strong> about 850MW of data center capacity delivered in the quarter. Co-CEO Clay Magouyrk said the bottleneck has moved from chips to power to buildings.</li><li><strong>Margins:</strong> cloud and software margin fell to 55% from 60%; the CFO said the step-down was expected and should level off over the next couple of years.</li><li><strong>Old business:</strong> traditional software revenue fell 3%.</li><li><strong>Since the report (investor day, Oct 28; third-party report, not checked against Oracle's own materials):</strong> FY2030 targets of at least $225B revenue and at least $21 EPS; capital spending peaking in FY27–28; free cash flow positive in the second half of FY28; new market funding for FY27–30 capped at $20B.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Negative free cash flow.</strong> Minus $5.4B in the quarter, with FY27 capital spending of $90–95B gross (net cash no more than $70B).</li><li><strong>Lease commitments.</strong> $288B, mostly data centers, not yet on the balance sheet.</li><li><strong>Permits and the grid.</strong> New Mexico and Wisconsin sites face delays that management said do not affect FY27 guidance.</li><li><strong>Margin.</strong> 55% from 60%, with supply-chain and energy costs named in the filing.</li><li><strong>Customer concentration.</strong> The share of backlog from any one customer was not disclosed in what was read. Reuters reported in August that almost half of the then-$638B backlog was OpenAI.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-funding-the-build-both-ways\">The big worry: funding the build, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Sticking to the investor-day plan: no more than $20B of new market funding through FY30 and free cash flow turning positive in the second half of FY28.</li><li>Customer prepayments and customer- or vendor-supplied hardware carrying more of the cost, as $11.4B of prepayments did last quarter.</li><li>Sites switching on. New Mexico's air-permit decision is due by Nov 23, 2026 (Capacity, third-party).</li><li>Renewals at higher prices: about 20% above the original contracts.</li><li>A lower rate path. No Fed official is arguing for cuts now, so this one is not on the table soon.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>The cost of money. Oracle's Nov 2 raise was reported as $10B of bonds with coupons of 7% to 8.65% and $10B of stock at $116, an 11% discount (The Next Web, third-party). That matches the whole $20B cap, five days after it was announced.</li><li>A rating cut to junk. S&amp;P cut Oracle to BBB-, the lowest investment-grade rung, in July; Moody's is Baa2 with a negative outlook (Reuters via The Star, Aug 5).</li><li>A hiking Fed: a December hike is about 90% priced and the 10-year is near 5%.</li><li>Site delays. Oracle sent a force majeure notice on the New Mexico project and its gas pipeline slipped to February 2027 (Capacity); the Wisconsin campus could see partial power slip to about June 2028 (TechRadar). Both are third-party reports.</li><li>Its largest customer stumbling. The Wall Street Journal reported in April that OpenAI missed user and revenue targets against about $600B of compute commitments (per Implicator, third-party).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How this company is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>The weakest balance sheet of the five cloud names: the only one rated near the bottom of investment grade, and the only one whose 5-year credit insurance cost hit an 18-year high this year (215 basis points, Reuters via The Star).</li><li>Its suppliers are helping pay: Nvidia and Broadcom both disclose guarantees and financing backstops for customers' builds in their latest filings.</li><li>Against peers: Microsoft funds from cash flow; Alphabet and Amazon borrow at AA-level ratings. Oracle pays far more for the same dollar.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Capital spending:</strong> FY27 $90–95B gross, net cash no more than $70B. FY28: the investor day put the peak in FY27–28 (third-party report).</li><li><strong>Shares:</strong> a $20B at-the-market program was fully used in the quarter; a further $10B of stock was reported sold on Nov 2. No buybacks.</li><li><strong>Dividend:</strong> $0.50 a share.</li><li><strong>Debt:</strong> $125.0B of notes and borrowings at quarter end (press figures differ: $117B to $129.5B), before the reported $10B November bond sale.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> two co-CEOs since Sept 22, 2025: Clay Magouyrk and Mike Sicilia. Neither is a founder. Safra Catz moved to executive vice chair.</li><li><strong>Founder:</strong> Larry Ellison is executive chair, chief technology officer and the largest stockholder. Chair and CEO are split on paper, but the chair is an executive. The lead independent director is Michael Boskin.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> Hilary Maxson, since April 6, 2026, hired from outside. The prior finance chief moved to a sales role.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> the co-CEO appointments are described as long-term succession planning. No successor to Ellison's role is named.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> one share class found. Ellison's exact stake: not found in this run. The 2026 proxy says part of his shares are pledged and that he is exempt from the company's anti-pledging policy.</li><li><strong>Recent changes:</strong> new co-CEOs (14 months) and a new CFO (7 months) during the largest funding programme in the company's history. Annual meeting Nov 18, 2026. Investigations: not found.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> a great deal. The founder sets the direction from the chair, is the largest holder and has pledged stock, while the people running the company and its finances day to day are all new in their seats.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"ORCL\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"ORCL · daily · my levels chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">ORCL · daily · my levels</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"374.0\" y2=\"374.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"289.0\" y2=\"289.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"204.0\" y2=\"204.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"119.0\" y2=\"119.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"34.0\" y2=\"34.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"10.3\" x2=\"10.3\" y1=\"50.7\" y2=\"78.4\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"8.9\" y=\"54.4\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"16.9\" fill=\"#A63A2E\" class=\"c-dn\"/><line x1=\"14.9\" x2=\"14.9\" y1=\"46.6\" y2=\"111.3\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"65.4\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"23.1\" fill=\"#A63A2E\" class=\"c-dn\"/><line x1=\"19.6\" x2=\"19.6\" y1=\"69.4\" y2=\"110.2\" stroke=\"#2F6E4A\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-up\"/><rect x=\"18.1\" y=\"101.4\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"7.9\" fill=\"#2F6E4A\" class=\"c-up\"/><line x1=\"24.2\" x2=\"24.2\" y1=\"148.4\" y2=\"175.4\" st