 line at $132.46, then the 50% line at $139.64 next to the 50-day, then the 61.8% line at $146.82, which matches the October highs ($146.40 and $148.48).</li><li><strong>Floor:</strong> $109.21, the Nov 6 low. The reported $116 share-sale price sits between.</li><li><strong>Your tested edge:</strong> does not apply. This is a downtrend and RSI is not under 40. Your theme map carries ORCL on the put side (AI debt); &quot;downtrend plus RSI over 60&quot; is the setup you have found works only on TSLA, and RSI is 49 anyway.</li><li><strong>Scorecard:</strong> no open or watching play.</li><li><strong>Verdict:</strong> the chart supports the downside until proven otherwise. It disagrees with management's raised targets; the market is pricing the funding, not the backlog. A close above $139.64 would say the bounce is real and a close above $148.48 would flip the lean; a close below $109.21 confirms the down-move.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-would-change-this-read\">What would change this read</h3>\n<ul><li>The next report, about Dec 14: cloud growth against 65–71%, free cash flow, the first full statement of 2027 spending among the big builders, and whether more money is being raised beyond the stated cap.</li><li>Rating agencies: any move by Moody's off its negative outlook, in either direction.</li><li>Nov 23: the New Mexico air-permit decision. Dec 9: the Fed.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"confidence-and-sources\">Confidence and sources</h3>\n<p>Solid: the September results and guidance (company release, 10-Q). Thin: everything since. The investor-day targets and the Nov 2 raise come from third-party pages, not Oracle's own documents; Ellison's stake, the single-customer share of today's backlog and a confirmed report date were not found.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://investor.oracle.com/investor-news/news-details/2026/Oracle-Announces-Q1-Results-Driven-by-Triple-Digit-Growth-in-Cloud-Infrastructure-Revenues/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Q1 release</a> · <a href=\"https://www.